[[:db/cas 17592186065338 :tutorial/content "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= 5^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 5^6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} \\\\[5pt]\n&= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} \\\\[5pt]\n&= 5^6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above."] [:db/cas 17592186065339 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} &= {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} \\\\\n&= {x}^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064680 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({x}^{3})}^{2} &= {x}^{3\\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485550 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the exponents and then we calculate the resulting power of \n$2.$\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({2}^{2})}^{3} & = \\\\ {2}^{(2\\cdot 3)} & = \\\\ {2}^{6} & = \\\\ 64\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we multiply the exponents and then we calculate the resulting power of \n$2.$\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({2}^{2})}^{3} &= {2}^{(2\\cdot 3)} \\\\\n&= {2}^{6} \\\\\n&= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063922 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(x^4\\right)^5\t&= \\\n\tx^{4\\cdot5} \t&= \\\n\tx^{20}\n}\n$$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^4\\right)^5 &= x^{4\\cdot5} \\\\\n&= x^{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485517 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({y}^{4})}^{-2} & = \\\\ {y}^{4\\cdot (-2)} & = \\\\ {y}^{-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-8.$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({y}^{4})}^{-2} &= {y}^{4\\cdot (-2)} \\\\\n&= {y}^{-8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485563 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({z}^{6})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {z}^{6\\cdot (-3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{-18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({z}^{6})}^{-3} &= {z}^{6\\cdot (-3)} \\\\\n&= {z}^{-18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544867 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{-3})}^{5} & = \\\\ {x}^{(-3)5} & = \\\\ {x}^{-15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-15.$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({x}^{-3})}^{5} &= {x}^{(-3)5} \\\\\n&= {x}^{-15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-15.$"] [:db/cas 17592186544871 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression inside the parenthesis and then we apply the power rule\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({p}^{0}{q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {(1\\cdot {q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {({q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {q}^{(-3)(-3)} & = \\\\ {q}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we simplify the expression inside the parenthesis and then we apply the power rule\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({p}^{0}{q}^{-3})}^{-3} &= {(1\\cdot {q}^{-3})}^{-3} \\\\\n&= {({q}^{-3})}^{-3} \\\\\n&= {q}^{(-3)(-3)} \\\\\n&= {q}^{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063923 :question/worked-solution "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6}&=\\\n\tx^{(-3)(-6)}&=\\\n\tx^{18}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore $n=18.$" "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6} &= x^{(-3)(-6)} \\\\\n&= x^{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $n=18.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485536 :question/problem "The equivalent radical expression for ${({(\\frac{2}{u})}^{1/4})}^{7/3}$ is" "The equivalent radical expression for ${({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4})}^{7/3}$ is"] [:db/cas 17592186485536 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({(\\frac{2}{u})}^{1/4})}^{7/3} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{u})}^{(1/4)(7/3)} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{u})}^{7/12} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[12]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4})}^{7/3} &= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{(1/4)(7/3)} \\\\\n&= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7/12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[12]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484829 :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{28}}" "\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{28}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484893 :answer/value "\\sqrt[12]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{7}}" "\\sqrt[12]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484910 :answer/value "\\sqrt[28]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{3}}" "\\sqrt[28]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484978 :answer/value "\\sqrt[7]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{6}}" "\\sqrt[7]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{6}}"] [:db/cas 17592186485318 :answer/value "\\sqrt[7]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{12}}" "\\sqrt[7]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{12}}"] [:db/cas 17592186485539 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({r}^{3/2})}^{1/4} & = \\\\ {r}^{(3/2)(1/4)} & = \\\\ {r}^{3/8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[8]{{r}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({r}^{3/2})}^{1/4} &= {r}^{(3/2)(1/4)} \\\\\n&= {r}^{3/8} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[8]{{r}^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544852 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ {({x}^{3})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {x}^{3\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $n=\\frac{3}{5}.$" "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} &= {({x}^{3})}^{1/5} \\\\\n&= {x}^{3\\cdot (1/5)} \\\\\n&= {x}^{3/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $n=\\frac{3}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186551254 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}} & = \\\\ {({(4b)}^{2})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}} &= {({(4b)}^{2})}^{1/5} \\\\\n&= {(4b)}^{2\\cdot (1/5)} \\\\\n&= {(4b)}^{2/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063924 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{-8}} & = \\\\ ({x}^{-8})^{1/4} & = \\\\ {x}^{(-8)\\cdot (1/4)} & = \\\\ {x}^{-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{-8}} &= ({x}^{-8})^{1/4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{(-8)\\cdot (1/4)} \\\\\n&= {x}^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233088 :question/worked-solution "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} & = \\\\ {t}^{-7/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} \\\\\n&= {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} \\\\\n&= {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} \\\\\n&= {t}^{-7/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485504 :question/worked-solution "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{t}^{3}}{s}=\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule followed by the quotient rule \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}^{12\\cdot 3}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}^{36}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ {a}^{36-4} & = \\\\ {a}^{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{t}^{3}}{s}=\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule followed by the quotient rule \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}} &= \\frac{{a}^{12\\cdot 3}}{{a}^{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{{a}^{36}}{{a}^{4}} \\\\\n&= {a}^{36-4} \\\\\n&= {a}^{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485511 :question/worked-solution "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}=\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply power rule followed by the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{b}^{9\\cdot 3}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{b}^{27}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ {b}^{27-2} & = \\\\ {b}^{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}=\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply power rule followed by the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}} &= \\frac{{b}^{9\\cdot 3}}{{b}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{{b}^{27}}{{b}^{2}} \\\\\n&= {b}^{27-2} \\\\\n&= {b}^{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544805 :question/worked-solution "First, we express the radical as a fractional power and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{-6/5}} & = \\\\ {({z}^{-6/5})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {z}^{(-6/5)(1/3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{-2/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" "First, we express the radical as a fractional power and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{-6/5}} &= {({z}^{-6/5})}^{1/3} \\\\\n&= {z}^{(-6/5)(1/3)} \\\\\n&= {z}^{-2/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186063925 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/3}})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {({x}^{-1/3})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{(-1/3)\\cdot (4)}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule, adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot {x}^{1} & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3+1} & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3+3/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{-1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $k=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot x &= {\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/3}}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot x \\\\\n&= {({x}^{-1/3})}^{4}\\cdot x \\\\\n&= {x}^{(-1/3)\\cdot (4)}\\cdot x \\\\\n&= {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule, adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x &= {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot {x}^{1} \\\\\n&= {x}^{-4/3+1} \\\\\n&= {x}^{-4/3+3/3} \\\\\n&= {x}^{-1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $k=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/ensure 17592186063922 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186063923 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186063924 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186063925 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186064680 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186233088 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186485504 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186485511 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186485517 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186485536 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186485539 :question/validate] [:db/ensure